\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Toy spectra scored the way an analysis would score them, at the recommended working point. \textbf  {Left:} the A-half background model is locally wrong, so the excess exists only in the estimator and stage B removes it. \textbf  {Right:} the background model is wrong for both halves, so the excess is real in the data and confirms. Only the first of these is a fluctuation in the sense the split can act on.\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:abspec}{{12}{23}{Toy spectra scored the way an analysis would score them, at the recommended working point. \textbf {Left:} the A-half background model is locally wrong, so the excess exists only in the estimator and stage B removes it. \textbf {Right:} the background model is wrong for both halves, so the excess is real in the data and confirms. Only the first of these is a fluctuation in the sense the split can act on.\relax }{figure.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Matched-robustness comparison at $f = 0.30$, $Z_{\mathrm  {cut}} = 3$, $N = 6616$, for a defect rate $\epsilon = 10^{-3}$ ($\approx 1$ artefact above $3\sigma $ per scan). A positive gain means the split reaches a \emph  {weaker} signal than a single-stage scan that is equally safe.\relax }}{24}{table.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:abnoise}{{14}{24}{Matched-robustness comparison at $f = 0.30$, $Z_{\mathrm {cut}} = 3$, $N = 6616$, for a defect rate $\epsilon = 10^{-3}$ ($\approx 1$ artefact above $3\sigma $ per scan). A positive gain means the split reaches a \emph {weaker} signal than a single-stage scan that is equally safe.\relax }{table.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Matched-robustness advantage of the split as a function of the coherent fraction $\rho $ of the estimator's outliers, at three defect rates. The dotted line is the pure cost of splitting with a perfect estimator. The break-even is essentially rate-independent, so the criterion is a property of the estimator's failure modes rather than of how often they occur.\relax }}{25}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:abnoise}{{13}{25}{Matched-robustness advantage of the split as a function of the coherent fraction $\rho $ of the estimator's outliers, at three defect rates. The dotted line is the pure cost of splitting with a perfect estimator. The break-even is essentially rate-independent, so the criterion is a property of the estimator's failure modes rather than of how often they occur.\relax }{figure.caption.39}{}}
